   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>it is in a read only section of the process's address space because the string literal is a constant. (the <code>str</code> variable is not in read only space but it should be made to point to a valid writable location before you can use it the way you do.)</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 29093883,
   "parent_id": 19600526,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 65295,
   "author_display_name": "Seth",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/19600526/#comment29093883_19600526",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2013-10-25T22:50:45Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "The problem is that you shouldn&#39;t assign a string literal to a <code>char *</code>, it&#39;s deprecated, and your compiler should have generated a warning. See: <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/13248399/hello-world-string-literal-can-be-assigned-to-char-type\" title=\"hello world string literal can be assigned to char type\">stackoverflow.com/questions/13248399/&hellip;</a>"
  },
